course: dictionary
lesson: 2986
title: Informal words and slang
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to casual talk, the way people chat with friends and family. Writers use them in stories to make speech sound real. «This bike is duff, it keeps falling apart» tells you the bike is bad, not that it is a kind of pudding.
explain: Duff is informal and mostly used in the UK. The words around it show its meaning: a duff battery, duff advice, a duff note. Ask yourself what would fit here, then try the simple meaning, bad or not working, and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Slang can change its meaning from place to place. In America, your duff can mean your bottom, so «get off your duff» means stop being lazy. Check where the story is set and whether the words sound like friendly chat or like a dictionary.
fact: Duff (informal, UK) means bad, useless or not working.
fact: «A duff battery» is a battery that does not work; «duff advice» is advice that is poor or wrong.
fact: «Get off your duff» (slang, mainly US) means stop being lazy and get moving.
fact: «Duff up» (slang, UK) means to beat someone up, so it is not a friendly phrase.
fact: A duff is also an old-fashioned word for a steamed pudding, as in plum duff.
exercise: «The torch is duff, so we need new batteries.» What does duff mean here? | *Bad, not working | Bright and shiny | Covered in plum jam | Very heavy
exercise: Grandad says, «Get off your duff and help me with the washing!» What does he want? | Go to bed early | Sit down and have a rest | Run to the shops | *Stop being lazy and get up to help
exercise: A boy says, «My drawing is duff, so I'll start again.» What does duff suggest? | *Poor or bad, so he wants to try again | Sweet and sticky, like a pudding | Very good and finished | Made of wood
test: Nadia's music teacher says she played a duff note. What is a duff note? | A note in a song about pudding | *A wrong or badly played note | A note sung very quietly | A high, clear note
test: Why do writers use slang like «duff» in a story? | *It makes characters sound like real people talking casually | It is only used in maths books | It is always the same as the dictionary meaning | It makes the story longer
exam: In a story, a boy says, «This kite is duff, the string keeps snapping.» Which meaning of duff fits best? | *Bad or not working | Your bottom | A kind of pudding | To beat someone up
